\subsection{\code{BaseRSpikeLayerProcessor$extract_data()}}{
  Read the spikes as one flat list of points.

A line layer's \code{data} is a list of \emph{series}, because several lines can
share one layer. A lollipop is read as a bar is, and a bar layer's
\code{data} is one point per position -- so the single series the inherited
extraction produces is unwrapped here rather than shipped one level
too deep, where the frontend would read the whole chart as a single
point.

Only the first series is taken. \code{plot(type = "h")} and
\code{lines(type = "h")} each draw exactly one, and \code{matplot} -- the call
that draws several -- has its own dispatch that never reaches here.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRSpikeLayerProcessor$extract_data(layer_info)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    List of \code{x}/\code{y} points, empty when there is nothing to read
  }
}

